 ],
 "literal": "Did you see? Or did you hear from those who saw? / Speak, so that we may clearly know one thing, we who desire! / Where white-tusked elephants bathe in the Sone river, / to obtain gold-abounding Pataliputra, / from whose mouth did you hear of the return of our lover?",
 "translation": "Did you see him yourself, or did you hear it from those who saw him? Speak, so that we who yearn may know the truth clearly! From whose mouth did you hear the news of our lover's return—he who went to acquire wealth as vast as the gold-abounding city of Pataliputra, where white-tusked elephants bathe in the Sone river?",
 "modern": "நீயே அவரைப் பார்த்தாயா? அல்லது பார்த்தவர்களிடம் கேட்டாயா? நாங்கள் உண்மையை தெளிவாகத் தெரிந்துகொள்ள விரும்புகிறோம், சொல்வாயாக! வெண்மையான தந்தங்களை உடைய யானைகள் சோணை நதியில் நீராடும், பொன் வளம் மிக்க பாடலிபுத்திர நகரத்தைப் போன்ற பெரும் செல்வத்தைப் பெறுவதற்காகச் சென்ற நம் காதலரின் வருகையைப் பற்றி நீ யார் சொல்லக் கேட்டாய்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her friend or a messenger who has brought news of the hero's return. Having suffered long in separation, she is filled with both hope and disbelief, demanding to know if the messenger saw him firsthand or heard it from others. The poem employs a grand historical and geographical allusion to the wealthy city of Pataliputra on the banks of the Sone river, where elephants bathe. This imagery emphasizes the immense wealth the hero went to acquire and elevates the scale of his successful quest. The tension between her deep desire and her need for absolute certainty drives the emotional core of the poem. Key words include 'காதலர் வரவே' (the return of the lover) and 'ஒன்று தெளிய' (to clarify one thing).",
